\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$. Specialized notation and index ranges follow the cited source and the definitions below.


$\sigma_k(x)_2=\inf\{\lVert x-z\rVert_2:z\text{ has at most }k\text{ nonzero coordinates}\}$ is the best $k$-term approximation error. $\Phi$ maps $N$-dimensional signals to $n$ measurements and $\Delta$ is a reconstruction map.
\paragraph{Record scope and qualifications.}
The catalogue title is retained as metadata. Named certificates, contracts, bounds or frameworks in that title are not asserted as results of the original source extract.
The measurement distribution and whether probability is uniform over all signals are not specified; the unusual supplied sparsity range is preserved without correction.

Source-record limitation: The measurement ensemble/uniformity is absent and the supplied n log(N/n) sparsity range has unresolved extraction concerns; no corrected recovery target is introduced.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
In compressed sensing, suppose the $\ell^2$ instance-optimal recovery estimate $\lVert x-\Delta(\Phi x)\rVert_2\leq C_0\sigma_k(x)_2$ holds for $k\leq cn\log(N/n)$ with probability at least $1-e^{-bn}$. Can one construct a decoder $\Delta$ with the same guarantee and low algorithmic complexity? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2007/36}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this source record open; that metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. The label is preserved for provenance and is not an independent verification of present open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-21.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Construct a fast compressed-sensing decoder with the same stated high-probability recovery guarantee.

